Rem ==================================================================
Rem alg.shoddy — test program for machines/alg.shoddy
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the csvtest / fin / eng / lin pattern: it prints
Rem what it is doing, stops at the first failed assertion, and ends with
Rem ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when none fail.
Rem
Rem BY PROPERTY FAR MORE THAN BY VALUE, because most of this machine's
Rem answers are not unique. There are several correct factorisations of
Rem the same polynomial, several correct antiderivatives, and many correct
Rem ways to write the same minimal form. So:
Rem
Rem   * integration is checked by DIFFERENTIATING THE RESULT BACK
Rem   * factorisation is checked by EXPANDING THE RESULT
Rem   * partial fractions are checked by RECOMBINING THEM
Rem   * the parser and printer are checked against EACH OTHER
Rem
Rem Asserting a particular string for any of those would pin an
Rem implementation detail and break on the first improvement.
Rem
Rem THE ORDER IS TESTED FIRST AND DIRECTLY, before anything that depends
Rem on it. If AlgCompare is not a total order then the sort inside AlgPass
Rem is unstable, the fixpoint oscillates, and the mill spins until the cap
Rem fires — at which point the error blames the cap and not the order.
Rem Twenty lines here save a day there.
Rem
Rem This file includes BOTH alg and eng, which is allowed and is the only
Rem place the bridge can be tested: neither machine includes the other, so
Rem neither one's own suite can prove they compose. A test file is a mill
Rem and mills include what they need.
Rem
Rem What cannot be tested here: the aborting guards. Shoddy has no
Rem catchable errors, so AlgRat(1, 0), an unbound variable in AlgEvalAt,
Rem the AlgSimplify fixpoint cap, AlgCallOf on an unknown function and
Rem AlgExpandPow above its bound all kill the program rather than failing
Rem an assertion. Each is verified by hand and named here instead.
Rem Everything returning a Result IS assertable on both arms, which is the
Rem argument for drawing the line where it is drawn.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/alg.shoddy"
Include "../machines/eng.shoddy"
Rem The corpus assertions fold All over lists, and All is seq's. alg
Rem includes seq but does not export it, so this file asks for it itself —
Rem which is the v1.6.0 scoping working exactly as intended.
Include "../machines/seq.shoddy"

Def X() As AlgEx
    AlgVarOf("x")

Def Y() As AlgEx
    AlgVarOf("y")

Def N(k As Number) As AlgEx
    AlgRat(k, 1)

Def P(s As String) As AlgEx
    AlgParse(s)

Rem The corpus: at least one of every variant, so a missing Case in any
Rem walker shows up as an abort rather than as a wrong answer in
Rem somebody's mill three months later.
Def Corpus() As List Of AlgEx
    { N(0), N(1), AlgRat(0 - 3, 4), X(), Y(),
      P("x + 1"), P("x * y"), P("x ^ 2"), P("2 * x + 3 * y"),
      P("sin(x)"), P("exp(2 * x + 1)"), P("1 / x"),
      P("x ^ 3 - 2 * x - 5"), P("(x + 1) * (x - 1)"),
      P("sin(x) / (1 + cos(x))"), P("2/3 * x + 1/6") }

Def Close(a As Number, b As Number, tol As Number) As Boolean
    Abs(a - b) <= tol

Def Main()
    Print("--- THE ORDER IS TOTAL (FIRST, BECAUSE EVERYTHING RESTS ON IT) ---")
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(a) => AlgCompare(a, a) = 0), "REFLEXIVE")
    Assert(All(Corpus(),
               Fn(a) => All(Corpus(),
                            Fn(b) => AlgCompare(a, b) = 0 - AlgCompare(b, a))),
           "ANTISYMMETRIC OVER THE WHOLE CORPUS")
    Rem Transitivity over every triple: if a <= b and b <= c then a <= c.
    Assert(All(Corpus(),
               Fn(a) => All(Corpus(),
                            Fn(b) => All(Corpus(),
                                         Fn(c) => Not (AlgCompare(a, b) <= 0
                                                       And AlgCompare(b, c) <= 0)
                                                  Or AlgCompare(a, c) <= 0))),
           "TRANSITIVE OVER EVERY TRIPLE")
    Assert(All(Corpus(),
               Fn(a) => All(Corpus(),
                            Fn(b) => AlgCompare(a, b) <> 0 Or AlgEqual(a, b))),
           "AND COMPARING EQUAL IMPLIES BEING EQUAL")

    Print("--- EVERY WORD SURVIVES EVERY VARIANT ---")
    Rem The test that catches a missing Case. If any walker has five of
    Rem six branches it aborts here rather than in a mill.
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => AlgDepth(AlgSimplify(e)) >= 1), "SIMPLIFY AND DEPTH")
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => AlgSize(e) >= 1), "SIZE")
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => Len(AlgShow(e)) > 0), "SHOW")
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => Len(AlgShowFull(e)) > 0), "SHOWFULL")
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => Len(AlgTree(e)) > 0), "TREE")
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => AlgDepth(AlgDiff(e, "x")) >= 1), "DIFFERENTIATE")
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => AlgDepth(AlgExpand(e)) >= 1), "EXPAND")
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => Length(AlgVarsOf(e)) >= 0), "VARSOF")

    Print("--- EXACT RATIONALS ---")
    Assert(AlgNumOf(AlgRat(4, 0 - 6)) = 0 - 2, "SIGN MOVES TO THE NUMERATOR")
    Assert(AlgDenOf(AlgRat(4, 0 - 6)) = 3, "AND THE GCD IS DIVIDED OUT")
    Rem The assertion that separates a CAS from a calculator.
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgRatAdd(AlgExact(1, 3), AlgExact(1, 6)), AlgExact(1, 2)),
           "ONE THIRD PLUS ONE SIXTH IS EXACTLY ONE HALF")
    Assert(AlgDenOf(AlgSimplify(P("1/3 + 1/6"))) = 2, "AND THROUGH THE PARSER TOO")
    Rem The simplest rational within tolerance, not the double's exact
    Rem binary value 3602879701896397/36028797018963968.
    Assert(AlgDenOf(AlgOf(0.1)) = 10, "0.1 RATIONALISES TO A TENTH")
    Assert(AlgDenOf(AlgOf(0.25)) = 4, "AND 0.25 TO A QUARTER")
    Assert(AlgNumOf(AlgOf(0 - 0.5)) = 0 - 1, "AND A NEGATIVE KEEPS ITS SIGN")
    Assert(AlgToNumber(AlgRatMul(AlgExact(2, 3), AlgExact(3, 2))) = 1, "MULTIPLICATION")
    Assert(AlgRatCmp(AlgExact(1, 3), AlgExact(1, 2)) = 0 - 1, "COMPARISON")

    Print("--- SIMPLIFICATION ---")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgAddOf(X(), X()), AlgMulOf(N(2), X())), "X PLUS X IS TWO X")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgMulOf(X(), X()), AlgPowOf(X(), N(2))), "X TIMES X IS X SQUARED")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgAddOf(X(), AlgZero()), X()), "ADDING ZERO")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgMulOf(X(), AlgOne()), X()), "MULTIPLYING BY ONE")
    Assert(AlgIsZero(AlgSimplify(AlgMulOf(X(), AlgZero()))), "MULTIPLYING BY ZERO")
    Assert(AlgIsZero(AlgSimplify(AlgSubOf(X(), X()))), "X MINUS X IS ZERO")
    Assert(AlgIsOne(AlgSimplify(AlgDivOf(X(), X()))), "X OVER X IS ONE")
    Assert(AlgEqual(P("2 * x + 3 * x"), P("5 * x")), "LIKE TERMS COLLECT")
    Assert(AlgEqual(P("x ^ 0"), AlgOne()), "ANYTHING TO THE ZERO")
    Rem The assertion that proves the canonical order is doing its job.
    Assert(AlgEqual(P("x + y"), P("y + x")), "SUMS COMMUTE INVISIBLY")
    Assert(AlgEqual(P("x * y"), P("y * x")), "AND SO DO PRODUCTS")
    Rem No sum or product may ever hold fewer than two elements.
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => AlgTwoOrMore(AlgSimplify(e))),
           "THE TWO-OR-MORE INVARIANT HOLDS ACROSS THE CORPUS")

    Print("--- PARSING AND PRECEDENCE ---")
    Assert(AlgToNumber(P("2^3^2")) = 512, "POWER IS RIGHT-ASSOCIATIVE, SO 512 NOT 64")
    Assert(AlgEqual(P("-x^2"), AlgNegOf(AlgPowOf(X(), N(2)))),
           "AND UNARY MINUS IS LOOSER THAN POWER")
    Assert(AlgEqual(P("2 + 3 * 4"), N(14)), "TIMES BINDS TIGHTER THAN PLUS")
    Assert(AlgEqual(P("(2 + 3) * 4"), N(20)), "AND BRACKETS OVERRIDE IT")

    Print("--- PRINTING RECONSTRUCTS WHAT THE TYPE DOES NOT HAVE ---")
    Assert(AlgShow(AlgSubOf(AlgVarOf("a"), AlgVarOf("b"))) = "a - b",
           "SUBTRACTION, NOT a + -1 * b")
    Assert(AlgShow(AlgDivOf(AlgVarOf("a"), AlgVarOf("b"))) = "a / b",
           "DIVISION, NOT a * b ^ -1")
    Assert(AlgShow(AlgNegOf(AlgVarOf("a"))) = "-a", "UNARY MINUS")
    Assert(AlgShow(AlgSubOf(AlgVarOf("a"), AlgSubOf(AlgVarOf("b"), AlgVarOf("c"))))
           = "a - (b - c)", "A MINUS A DIFFERENCE KEEPS ITS BRACKETS")
    Assert(AlgShow(AlgDivOf(AlgVarOf("a"), AlgMulOf(AlgVarOf("b"), AlgVarOf("c"))))
           = "a / (b * c)", "AND SO DOES A DIVISION BY A PRODUCT")
    Assert(AlgShow(AlgNegOf(AlgAddOf(AlgVarOf("a"), AlgVarOf("b")))) = "-(a + b)",
           "AND MINUS A SUM")

    Print("--- THE ROUND TRIP (THE STRONGEST SINGLE TEST HERE) ---")
    Rem Parser, printer and simplifier checked against each other over the
    Rem whole corpus. Nothing else exercises all three at once.
    Assert(All(Corpus(), Fn(e) => AlgEqual(P(AlgShow(e)), e)),
           "EVERY CORPUS EXPRESSION SURVIVES SHOW THEN PARSE")

    Print("--- READING IS TOTAL AND CARRIES A POSITION ---")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("2 +* 3")), "A MALFORMED EXPRESSION IS AN ERR")
    Assert(AlgAtOf(AlgRead("2 +* 3")) > 0, "AND THE ERR CARRIES A TOKEN OFFSET")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("(1")), "AN UNCLOSED BRACKET")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("")), "AND AN EMPTY STRING - CLEANLY, NOT BY ABORTING")
    Assert(Not AlgIsErr(AlgRead("x + 1")), "WHILE A GOOD ONE READS")

    Print("--- INCLUDING A DIVISION BY ZERO, WHICH ONCE ENDED THE SESSION ---")
    Rem The parser was total for SYNTAX only. Its / branch called the
    Rem aborting AlgDivOf, so a typed "1/0" killed the mill rather than the
    Rem line. The builders fold the divisor before the parser sees it, so a
    Rem textual /0 is only the easiest of these shapes, not the whole set.
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("1/0")), "A LITERAL ZERO DIVISOR IS AN ERR")
    Assert(AlgAtOf(AlgRead("1/0")) = 3, "AND POINTS AT THE DIVISOR, TOKEN 3")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("1/(1-1)")), "A DIVISOR THAT FOLDS TO ZERO")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("x/(y-y)")), "EVEN ONE FOLDED OUT OF VARIABLES")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("sin(1/0)")), "AND ONE BURIED IN A CALL")
    Assert(Not AlgIsErr(AlgRead("1/x")), "WHILE AN HONEST DIVISION STILL READS")
    Assert(Not AlgIsErr(AlgRead("1/(x-x+1)")), "AND ONE THAT FOLDS TO SOMETHING ELSE")

    Print("--- 0 TO A NEGATIVE POWER IS THAT SAME QUESTION, SPELT OTHERWISE ---")
    Rem It used to answer 0 - quietly and wrongly - while 1/0 killed the
    Rem mill: two answers to one question, and neither of them right.
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("0^-1")), "A ZERO BASE UNDER A NEGATIVE POWER IS AN ERR")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgRead("0^-2")), "WHATEVER THE NEGATIVE EXPONENT")
    Assert(AlgWhyOf(AlgRead("0^-1")) = AlgWhyOf(AlgRead("1/0")),
           "AND THE TWO SPELLINGS REFUSE IN THE SAME WORDS")
    Assert(AlgIsZero(AlgOkOf(AlgRead("0^2"))), "A POSITIVE EXPONENT STILL FOLDS TO ZERO")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgRead("0^0")), AlgOne()), "AND 0^0 IS STILL ONE")
    Rem An exponent whose sign is unknown has no answer available, so the
    Rem power stands unevaluated rather than guessing one of the two.
    Assert(Not AlgIsErr(AlgRead("0^x")), "A SYMBOLIC EXPONENT NEITHER REFUSES")
    Assert(Not AlgIsZero(AlgOkOf(AlgRead("0^x"))), "NOR FOLDS TO ZERO ON A GUESS")

    Print("--- AND THE GUARDED TWINS ANSWER ON BOTH ARMS ---")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(TryAlgDivOf(AlgOne(), AlgZero())), "TRYALGDIVOF REFUSES A ZERO DIVISOR")
    Assert(Not AlgIsErr(TryAlgDivOf(AlgOne(), X())), "AND ANSWERS OK OTHERWISE")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(TryAlgPowOf(AlgZero(), AlgMinusOne())), "TRYALGPOWOF REFUSES 0^-1")
    Assert(Not AlgIsErr(TryAlgPowOf(AlgZero(), N(2))), "AND ANSWERS OK FOR 0^2")
    Rem The twin is the ONLY set of rules: the aborting form is defined
    Rem over it, so the two cannot drift apart.
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDivOf(AlgOne(), N(4)), AlgOkOf(TryAlgDivOf(AlgOne(), N(4)))),
           "AND ALGDIVOF IS ITS OK ARM WHEREVER IT ANSWERS AT ALL")

    Print("--- DIFFERENTIATION IS TOTAL ---")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(P("x^3"), "x"), P("3 * x^2")), "POWER RULE")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(P("sin(x)"), "x"), P("cos(x)")), "D SIN IS COS")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(P("x * sin(x)"), "x"), P("sin(x) + x * cos(x)")),
           "PRODUCT RULE, BY ALGEQUAL AND NOT BY STRING")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(P("sin(x^2)"), "x"), P("2 * x * cos(x^2)")), "CHAIN RULE")
    Rem The general case, which is why the constant-exponent shortcut was
    Rem not written on its own.
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(P("x^x"), "x"), P("x^x * (ln(x) + 1)")), "GENERAL POWER RULE")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiffN(P("x^3"), "x", 2), P("6 * x")), "SECOND DERIVATIVE")
    Assert(AlgIsZero(AlgDiff(P("y"), "x")), "A FREE VARIABLE DIFFERENTIATES TO ZERO")
    Assert(Length(AlgGrad(P("x^2 + y^2"), { "x", "y" })) = 2, "THE GRADIENT HAS ONE PER VARIABLE")

    Print("--- THE FUNCTION TABLES ARE IN STEP ---")
    Rem Iterating AlgFns means this cannot go stale when a function is
    Rem added. A function that differentiates but cannot evaluate breaks
    Rem AlgToFn; one that evaluates but cannot differentiate breaks AlgDiff.
    Assert(All(AlgFns(), Fn(f) => AlgDepth(AlgDiffFn(f, X())) >= 1),
           "EVERY FUNCTION HAS A DERIVATIVE")
    Assert(All(AlgFns(), Fn(f) => AlgEvalFn(f, 0.5) = AlgEvalFn(f, 0.5)),
           "AND EVERY FUNCTION EVALUATES")
    Assert(AlgKnowsFn("sin") And Not AlgKnowsFn("bessel"), "AND THE TABLE KNOWS ITS OWN EXTENT")

    Print("--- SYMBOLIC CONSTANTS ---")
    Rem pi is a SYMBOL, so this is exactly 0 and not 1.2246e-16.
    Assert(AlgIsZero(AlgCallOf("sin", AlgPiSym())), "SIN PI IS EXACTLY ZERO")
    Assert(AlgIsOne(AlgCallOf("cos", AlgZero())), "COS ZERO IS EXACTLY ONE")
    Assert(AlgIsOne(AlgCallOf("exp", AlgZero())), "EXP ZERO IS EXACTLY ONE")
    Assert(AlgIsOne(AlgCallOf("ln", AlgESym())), "LN E IS EXACTLY ONE")
    Rem And the value appears only at the evaluation boundary.
    Assert(Close(AlgEvalAt(AlgPiSym(), "z", 0), 3.14159265358979, 0.0000000001),
           "PI RESOLVES TO A NUMBER ONLY WHEN EVALUATED")

    Print("--- EXPANSION ---")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgExpand(P("(x + 1)^3")), P("x^3 + 3*x^2 + 3*x + 1")), "A BINOMIAL CUBE")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgExpand(P("(x + y) * (x - y)")), P("x^2 - y^2")),
           "A DIFFERENCE OF SQUARES")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgExpand(P("2 * (x + 3)")), P("2*x + 6")), "AND A CONSTANT MULTIPLE")

    Print("--- POLYNOMIALS ---")
    Assert(AlgPolyDegree(P("x^3 - 2*x - 5"), "x") = 3, "DEGREE")
    Assert(AlgPolyDegree(P("sin(x)"), "x") = 0 - 1, "AND A NON-POLYNOMIAL HAS NONE")
    Assert(AlgIsPoly(P("x^2 + 1"), "x"), "IS A POLYNOMIAL")
    Assert(Not AlgIsPoly(P("1/x"), "x"), "A NEGATIVE POWER IS NOT")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgCoeffAt(P("x^3 - 2*x - 5"), "x", 0), N(0 - 5)), "CONSTANT COEFFICIENT")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgCoeffAt(P("x^3 - 2*x - 5"), "x", 3), AlgOne()), "LEADING COEFFICIENT")
    Rem Lowest power first, so index k is the coefficient of x^k.
    Assert(Length(AlgCoeffList(AlgOkOf(AlgPolyCoeffs(P("x^3 - 2*x - 5"), "x")))) = 4,
           "A CUBIC HAS FOUR COEFFICIENTS")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgFromCoeffs(AlgCoeffList(AlgOkOf(AlgPolyCoeffs(P("x^3 - 2*x - 5"), "x"))), "x"),
                    P("x^3 - 2*x - 5")), "AND COEFFICIENTS ROUND TRIP")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgPolyDivide(P("x^2 - 1"), P("x - 1"), "x")), P("x + 1")),
           "POLYNOMIAL DIVISION")
    Assert(AlgIsZero(AlgOkOf(AlgPolyRem(P("x^2 - 1"), P("x - 1"), "x"))),
           "AND AN EXACT DIVISION LEAVES NOTHING")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgCommonFactor(P("2*x + 4*y")), P("2 * (x + 2*y)")), "COMMON FACTOR")

    Print("--- FACTORISATION, BY EXPANDING THE RESULT ---")
    Rem Never assert a factored FORM: 2*(x-1)*(x+1) and (2*x-2)*(x+1) are
    Rem both correct. Assert that expanding it gives back the input.
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgExpand(AlgOkOf(AlgFactor(P("x^2 - 5*x + 6"), "x"))),
                    AlgExpand(P("x^2 - 5*x + 6"))), "A QUADRATIC")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgExpand(AlgOkOf(AlgFactor(P("x^2 - 1"), "x"))),
                    AlgExpand(P("x^2 - 1"))), "A DIFFERENCE OF SQUARES")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgExpand(AlgOkOf(AlgFactor(P("x^3 - 6*x^2 + 11*x - 6"), "x"))),
                    AlgExpand(P("x^3 - 6*x^2 + 11*x - 6"))), "AND A CUBIC WITH THREE ROOTS")
    Rem The refusal is as important as the successes, and the reason has to
    Rem name the fact rather than say FAILED.
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgFactor(P("x^2 + 1"), "x")), "X SQUARED PLUS ONE DOES NOT FACTOR")
    Assert(Instr(AlgWhyOf(AlgFactor(P("x^2 + 1"), "x")), "IRREDUCIBLE") > 0,
           "AND THE REASON SAYS IRREDUCIBLE, NOT FAILED")

    Print("--- PARTIAL FRACTIONS, BY RECOMBINING THEM ---")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgTogether(AlgOkOf(AlgPartFrac(P("1/(x^2-1)"), "x"))),
                    P("1/(x^2-1)")), "A DECOMPOSITION RECOMBINES TO ITS INPUT")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgPartFrac(P("1/((x-1)*(x-1))"), "x")),
           "A REPEATED FACTOR IS REFUSED")
    Assert(Instr(AlgWhyOf(AlgPartFrac(P("1/((x-1)*(x-1))"), "x")), "REPEATED") > 0,
           "AND SAYS WHICH CASE IT IS")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgPartFrac(P("1/(x^2+1)"), "x")),
           "AND AN IRREDUCIBLE DENOMINATOR IS REFUSED TOO")

    Print("--- LIMITS ---")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgLimit(P("sin(x)/x"), "x", AlgZero())), AlgOne()),
           "SIN X OVER X AT ZERO IS ONE, BY L'HOPITAL")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgLimit(P("(x^2-1)/(x-1)"), "x", AlgOne())), N(2)),
           "AND THE REMOVABLE SINGULARITY AT ONE IS TWO")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgLimitInf(P("(2*x^2+1)/(x^2-3)"), "x")), N(2)),
           "EQUAL DEGREES AT INFINITY GIVE THE RATIO OF LEADING COEFFICIENTS")
    Assert(AlgIsZero(AlgOkOf(AlgLimitInf(P("1/x"), "x"))), "AND A SMALLER NUMERATOR GIVES ZERO")
    Rem The refusal that matters: a limit needing the SIDE of the approach.
    Rem This returned 0 before the check that catches it, which is exactly
    Rem the plausible wrong answer this machine must not produce.
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgLimit(P("abs(x)/x"), "x", AlgZero())),
           "ABS X OVER X AT ZERO IS REFUSED, NOT GUESSED")

    Print("--- INTEGRATION, BY DIFFERENTIATING IT BACK ---")
    Rem The property that catches every sign error and every missing
    Rem factor, and it covers exactly what AlgIntegRules claims.
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(AlgOkOf(AlgInteg(P("x^2"), "x")), "x"), P("x^2")), "A POWER")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(AlgOkOf(AlgInteg(P("1/x"), "x")), "x"), P("1/x")),
           "THE MINUS-ONE CASE, GIVING A LOGARITHM")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(AlgOkOf(AlgInteg(P("sin(x)"), "x")), "x"), P("sin(x)")), "SINE")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(AlgOkOf(AlgInteg(P("cos(x)"), "x")), "x"), P("cos(x)")), "COSINE")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(AlgOkOf(AlgInteg(P("exp(2*x)"), "x")), "x"), P("exp(2*x)")),
           "AND A LINEAR SUBSTITUTION")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgDiff(AlgOkOf(AlgInteg(P("sin(x) + 3*x"), "x")), "x"),
                    P("sin(x) + 3*x")), "A SUM WITH A CONSTANT MULTIPLE")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgIntegDef(P("x^2"), "x", AlgZero(), N(3))), N(9)),
           "AND THE DEFINITE INTEGRAL BY THE FUNDAMENTAL THEOREM")
    Rem The honest refusal: exp(x^2) has no elementary antiderivative and
    Rem no amount of engineering would find one.
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgInteg(P("exp(x^2)"), "x")), "A NON-ELEMENTARY INTEGRAL IS REFUSED")
    Assert(Instr(AlgWhyOf(AlgInteg(P("exp(x^2)"), "x")), "NON-LINEAR") > 0,
           "AND THE REASON NAMES THE NON-LINEAR ARGUMENT")
    Assert(Length(AlgIntegRules()) > 0, "AND THE TABLE DESCRIBES ITS OWN COVERAGE")

    Print("--- SOLVING, AND THE DISTINCTION THAT MATTERS ---")
    Assert(AlgEqual(Nth(AlgListOf(AlgSolve(P("2*x - 6"), "x")), 1), N(3)), "A LINEAR EQUATION")
    Assert(Length(AlgListOf(AlgSolve(P("x^2 - 5*x + 6"), "x"))) = 2, "A QUADRATIC HAS TWO ROOTS")
    Rem Exact and symbolic: sqrt(2), not 1.4142.
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgPowOf(Nth(AlgListOf(AlgSolve(P("x^2 - 2"), "x")), 1), N(2)), N(2)),
           "AND A SURD STAYS SYMBOLIC - ITS SQUARE IS EXACTLY TWO")
    Rem Ok({ }) means no solutions; Err means no method. Different facts.
    Assert(Length(AlgListOf(AlgSolve(P("x^2 + 1"), "x"))) = 0, "NO REAL ROOTS IS AN EMPTY OK")
    Assert(Not AlgIsErr(AlgSolve(P("x^2 + 1"), "x")), "AND IT IS AN OK, NOT AN ERR")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgSolve(P("sin(x)"), "x")), "WHILE NO METHOD IS AN ERR")
    Assert(AlgEqual(Nth(AlgListOf(AlgSolveFor(P("2*x"), P("6"), "x")), 1), N(3)),
           "AND SOLVEFOR MOVES THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE ACROSS")

    Print("--- SERIES, EXACTLY ---")
    Rem The visible payoff of exact rationals: 1/6 and 1/120, not 0.1667.
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgMaclaurin(P("sin(x)"), "x", 5)),
                    P("x - x^3/6 + x^5/120")), "THE MACLAURIN SERIES OF SINE TO ORDER FIVE")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgCoeffAt(AlgOkOf(AlgMaclaurin(P("sin(x)"), "x", 5)), "x", 3),
                    AlgExact(0 - 1, 6)), "AND THE CUBIC COEFFICIENT IS EXACTLY MINUS A SIXTH")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgCoeffAt(AlgOkOf(AlgMaclaurin(P("sin(x)"), "x", 5)), "x", 5),
                    AlgExact(1, 120)), "AND THE FIFTH IS EXACTLY ONE OVER 120")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgMaclaurin(P("exp(x)"), "x", 2)), P("1 + x + x^2/2")),
           "AND THE SERIES OF EXP")

    Print("--- FIRST-ORDER ODES ---")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgDeSolve(P("y"), "y", "x")), P("C * exp(x)")),
           "Y PRIME EQUALS Y GIVES C EXP X")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgOkOf(AlgDeSolve(P("2*x"), "y", "x")), P("x^2 + C")),
           "AND A PURE QUADRATURE INTEGRATES")
    Assert(AlgIsErr(AlgDeSolve(P("y^2 * x"), "y", "x")), "A GENERAL SEPARABLE ONE IS REFUSED")

    Print("--- SUBSTITUTION AND EVALUATION ---")
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgSubst(P("x^2 + 1"), "x", P("y + 1")), P("(y+1)^2 + 1")), "SUBSTITUTION")
    Rem Only a function the table already knows can appear in a parsed
    Rem expression at all, so AlgSubstFn rewrites those rather than
    Rem introducing user-defined ones — replacing sin by its own series is
    Rem the shape of use it is for.
    Assert(AlgEqual(AlgSubstFn(P("sin(x + 1)"), "sin", "u", P("u^2")), P("(x+1)^2")),
           "AND A FUNCTION CALL IS REPLACED BY ITS BODY")
    Assert(AlgEvalAt(P("x^2 + 1"), "x", 3) = 10, "EVALUATION AT A POINT")
    Assert(AlgEvalWith(P("x*y + 1"), { "x", "y" }, { 2, 5 }) = 11, "AND WITH SEVERAL VARIABLES")

    Print("--- THE BRIDGE TO ENG ---")
    Rem Neither machine includes the other, so this is the only place the
    Rem composition can be tested. A quotation is a builtin type, which is
    Rem exactly why they compose with no dependency in either direction.
    Assert(Call(AlgToFn(P("x^2"), "x"), 3) = 9, "AN EXPRESSION BECOMES A FUNCTION VALUE")
    Assert(Call(AlgToFn2(P("x*y"), "x", "y"), 3, 4) = 12, "AND A TWO-VARIABLE ONE TOO")
    Assert(Close(EngZeroOf(AlgToFn(P("x^3 - 2*x - 5"), "x"), 2, 3), 2.0945514815, 0.0000001),
           "AND ENGZEROOF SOLVES A FORMULA IT NEVER SAW COMPILED")
    Assert(Close(EngIntegrate(AlgToFn(P("x^2"), "x"), 0, 3, 100), 9, 0.0000001),
           "AND ENGINTEGRATE NUMERICALLY AGREES WITH ALGINTEGDEF")
    Assert(Close(EngMinOf(AlgToFn(P("(x-3)^2 + 1"), "x"), 0, 10), 3, 0.000001),
           "AND ENGMINOF FINDS THE MINIMUM OF A PARSED PARABOLA")

    Print("--- the tables are one value, not one per call ---")
    Rem A List compares by identity. While these were built by their Defs
    Rem each of these was False, and no machine could publish a canonical
    Rem list at all; they are held by a top-level Let now. AlgKnowsFn is
    Rem reached from every AlgCallOf, so this is also the difference
    Rem between reading a list and rebuilding one thousands of times.
    Assert(AlgFns() = AlgFns(), "ALGFNS IS THE SAME LIST EVERY CALL")
    Assert(AlgIntegRules() = AlgIntegRules(), "AND SO IS ALGINTEGRULES")
    Assert(AlgZero() = AlgZero() And AlgPiSym() = AlgPiSym(), "AND THE EXPRESSION CONSTANTS")

    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")
